Zsh, Oh My Zsh, and configuration

Install:
  Install missing Zsh and helper packages, Oh My Zsh and plugins; copy missing custom scripts and the theme, preserving existing copies.
  Back up and replace .zshrc with the bundled configuration; register Shell environment loading in .zshenv.
  Set /bin/zsh as the login shell when the Zsh package is newly installed.

Upgrade:
  Update installed packages, Oh My Zsh and plugins; back up and replace bundled custom scripts, the theme and .zshrc when Zsh configuration exists.
  Refresh Shell environment integration and preserve the login shell.

Status:
  Report package versions, Oh My Zsh and plugin Git revisions, and .zshrc presence.

Requirements:
  Ubuntu, APT and a regular user with sudo access.

Configuration:
  Shell configuration: ${ZDOTDIR:-$HOME}/.zshrc and ${ZDOTDIR:-$HOME}/.zshenv
  A custom ZDOTDIR must also be configured in the shell startup environment.
  Oh My Zsh: ZSH; default ~/.oh-my-zsh. Custom scripts and themes: ZSH_CUSTOM; default $ZSH/custom.
  Selected absolute paths are saved in .zshrc and ~/.config/wsley/zsh.sh; later operations reuse them unless environment variables override them.
  Open a new Zsh session after installation or configuration updates.
  show-size displays disk usage as a tree and supports minimum-size filtering; see show-size --help.
  The rm alias uses trash-cli; cleanup-dirs permanently removes caches and can also remove generated environments and build output; see cleanup-dirs --help.
